{"id": "sevt_01KneuKWmWeEDhkF1Dk1fsNT", "content": [{"text": "**Evidence**\n- Checkout deployed `6db1b9b` at 14:22:30. p99 was about 75ms until 14:21 and jumped to 3,404ms at 14:22. It then climbed to about 5,800ms by 14:59.\n- The other two deploys don't fit the timing. Cart `50293ce` went out at 14:21:05, a minute before the jump. Auth `67f6130` went out at 11:58.\n- The diff is labelled a readability refactor. It replaces one batched `order_id IN (...)` query plus an in-memory group with a per-order `SELECT ... WHERE order_id = ?` inside a loop. That is an N+1 query pattern.\n- The log shows it. The first per-row `order_items WHERE order_id = ?` query appears at 14:22:31, one second after the deploy. There are 3,787 such queries in the log, and they come in bursts under a single `request_id`. Only 89 `order_id IN` queries appear.\n\nI didn't check `db_pool_utilization` or the cart deploy further, because the checkout deploy explains the timing and the mechanism.\n\n**Fix:** revert `6db1b9b`, or restore the batched query.\n\n> **Root cause:** `6db1b9b` \u2014 the order summary refactor replaced the single batched `order_items` query with a per-order query inside a loop (N+1), so checkout latency scales with the number of open orders per request.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:51:20.946436Z", "type": "agent.message"}
